WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.380
With the MCU turning 22 films old upon the
release of Avengers: Endgame, many fans are

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:10.140
using their considerable expertise to guide
newcomers through Marvel's complex timeline.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.340
But taking into consideration story flow,
chronology, and plain common sense, just what

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.740
is the best way to enjoy the franchise?

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.640
With an emphasis on how all the movies link
together, and how to have the best viewing

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.900
experience possible, here's the order we think
is the best.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.300
Captain America: The First Avenger may be
the earliest setting the MCU has explored,

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.960
but the franchise was born with 2008's Iron
Man, and it was Robert Downey Jr. and his

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.100
genius billionaire playboy philanthropist
who became the beating heart of Marvel’s

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:39.600
narrative.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:44.940
Almost everything we associate with the MCU
begins here: the origin story of the flawed

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.700
but lovable hero, the graceful interweaving
of elements from the larger Marvel narrative,

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:54.020
and of course the compulsory post-credits
scene, teasing larger things to come.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:58.460
Even if it's not first in the timeline, the
birth of the narrative was Iron Man, and no

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:01.310
Marvel movie marathon should begin with anything
else.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:06.779
Iron Man 2 hit theaters two years after The
Incredible Hulk, but there are a couple of

00:01:06.779 --> 00:01:11.600
reasons why you should watch Tony and Rhodey
take out Whiplash's drones before Ed Norton's

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:12.920
lone MCU feature.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.499
First, although chronology isn’t the most
important thing to take into consideration

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.680
here, the events of Iron Man 2 mostly take
place before Incredible Hulk.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:26.209
Then again, judging by the news footage of
the Hulk's rampages that can seen in the background

00:01:26.209 --> 00:01:31.120
of the film's penultimate scene, there is
at least some overlap between the two movies'

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:31.620
timelines.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.319
Second, and most importantly, with Tony agreeing
to become a S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:01:36.319 --> 00:01:40.899
consultant at the end of Iron Man 2, watching
this movie first helps make sense of his appearance

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.340
at the end of the next one.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:48.280
Instead of a mid or post-credits scene, The
Incredible Hulk features a short final scene

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.380
which tied the events of the movie to the
larger Marvel narrative.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:53.820
"What if I told you we were putting a team
together?"

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:56.500
"Who's 'we'?"

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:03.159
Considering the tenor of Ross and Stark's
conversation, it seems likely that the original

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:07.669
plan was to follow the trajectory of the comics
and have the origin of the Avengers begin

00:02:07.669 --> 00:02:08.800
with a hunt for the Hulk.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.660
After plans changed, however, this scene stuck
out like a sore thumb.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:17.400
Marvel's solution was to release the short
film The Consultant on the Thor Blu-ray, which

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.460
we'll include as a necessary watch after Incredible
Hulk, but before Thor.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:26.680
It features two conversations between S.H.I.E.L.D.
agents Phil Coulson and Jasper Sitwell.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.900
One of the chats takes place before Incredible
Hulk's final scene…

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:34.020
"If we want the general to refuse this request,
then we have to send someone who will truly

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:34.520
annoy him."

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:38.080
And the other conversation takes place after
Incredible Hulk's final scene.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:42.500
"Apparently the Consultant outdid himself;
Ross got so mad he tried to have him removed

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:43.100
from the bar."

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:44.340
"How'd that go over?"

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:45.960
"Stark bought the place."

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:51.439
As a result, Tony Stark's fruitless conversation
with the General never goes anywhere exactly

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:55.500
as "planned" and fits nicely into MCU continuity
after all.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.540
Agent Coulson exits the story in Iron Man
2 when a S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:01.200
assignment calls him to New Mexico.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.280
We catch up with him after that film's credits
as he locates Thor's hammer in the desert,

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:10.500
marking the first time a Marvel post-credit
scene directly overlapped with an upcoming

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.000
film.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:15.480
It's also one of the reasons shifting Iron
Man 2's placement helps during a marathon

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.540
otherwise, Thor follows Iron Man 2 immediately.

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:23.100
This way, however, you get the tease of the
hammer at the end of Iron Man 2 and are made

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.080
to wait through Incredible Hulk for the tantalizing
payoff.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:30.740
It’s also worth considering that Thor and
Captain America should be watched back-to-back.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:35.600
Not only because they contrast wonderfully
in their settings and each kick off in Norway,

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:39.780
but also because the two movies work together
to launch the cosmic side of the MCU with

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.640
a basic concept: that science and magic could
be seen as "one and the same."

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:49.500
A lot of Marvel fans will tell you that Captain
America: The First Avenger should be watched

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:53.000
first, since most of its story takes place
first chronologically.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:57.740
But the movie is fine right where it is, coming
after Thor, and don't let anyone tell you

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:58.240
differently.

00:03:58.749 --> 00:04:02.040
To really understand this movie’s place
in the franchise, it’s important to take

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.880
into account what happens to Cap at the end
of the film.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:06.840
"You've been asleep, Cap.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:09.300
For almost seventy years."

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.040
Without the Iron Man, Hulk, and Thor films,
this ending just doesn't work!

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.000
That's why The First Avenger shouldn't be
the first movie in your marathon.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.760
But after Thor and before Avengers?

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:20.540
It's perfect.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:21.880
Speaking of which...

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:27.200
There’s something uniquely satisfying about
watching all five of the first MCU movies

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:30.660
and then seeing so many of those characters
unite in The Avengers.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:35.380
That satisfaction would work against a largely
chronological viewing of the films in a couple

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:36.240
of ways.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:40.880
The Avengers belongs right after Captain America
firstly because of the importance the Tesseract

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.680
plays in both, and because of how Cap's "man
out of time" theme informs his arc in The

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:46.180
Avengers.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.420
But a chronological viewing order would also
place 2019’s Captain Marvel in the number

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:56.880
two spot, and unless you haven’t noticed
Carol Danvers is nowhere to be seen in The

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:57.380
Avengers.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.560
Watching her origin story then experiencing
every Phase One hero except her come together

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:04.880
in The Avengers would be needlessly jarring.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.940
Although The Winter Soldier was actually the
third movie release in Phase Two, there's

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:12.280
at least one good reason to watch it first.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:16.820
One of the more valid criticisms of Marvel
Studios' second phase is that the Avengers

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:21.289
never really helped each other out, even as
each of the heroes found themselves in need

00:05:21.289 --> 00:05:21.780
of help.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:26.040
The Winter Soldier offers an explanation for
this issue, since the movie ends with the

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:30.160
disbandment of S.H.I.E.L.D., which brought
the Avengers together in the first place.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:34.560
Without Nick Fury there to bring them together,
coordination between the Avengers may have

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.180
been more difficult, hence Thor and Stark
going solo in their next appearances.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:43.280
The Dark World is probably best right after
The Winter Soldier.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.639
You can't push it too far down the order,
because it's the first time we see Loki answering

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:52.440
for his crimes against Earth, but it also
can't come immediately after The Avengers.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:56.900
After all, The Dark World shows us the aftermath
of Jane Foster’s discovery that Thor came

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:01.120
to Earth to fight the Chitauri but didn't
bother to drop her a line before or after.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:05.780
Coming so hot on the heels of The Avengers,
her frustration might seem a little strange.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:11.820
One of the most important aspects of Iron
Man 3 is Tony's space-induced PTSD, since

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.880
it explains just why Stark is willing to risk
so much in Age of Ultron.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:20.800
Giving two movies of space in between Avengers
and Iron Man 3 is helpful for this, since

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:23.620
it demonstrates that he's had some time for
his trauma to simmer.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.600
Placing it later in the viewing order than
The Winter Soldier would also explain why

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:31.919
the destruction of Tony’s home and kidnapping
of the President don’t rate highly on the

00:06:31.919 --> 00:06:32.919
S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:35.520
priority list, again, because they’re not
around anymore.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:41.099
There isn't much to tie Guardians of the Galaxy
to the rest of the MCU, so you might think

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.280
you could watch it at any point in your marathon.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:47.919
The only tangible connection was the brief
appearance of Thanos, who'd been introduced

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:49.520
during the credits of The Avengers.

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:54.000
But the Mad Titan is also the exact reason
why you should make sure to put Guardians

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.880
right after Iron Man 3 and before Age of Ultron
in your viewing order.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:03.440
"Fine, I'll do it myself."

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:08.620
This mid-credits scene from the Avengers'
first sequel marks the end of Thanos' strategy

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:11.960
of acquiring the Infinity Stones through intermediaries.

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:17.180
If Guardians doesn't stay before Age of Ultron,
Thanos' mid-credits proclamation doesn't make

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:17.840
sense.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:22.440
Avengers: Age of Ultron's spot is a pretty
solid no-brainer.

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:27.449
The dissolution of both S.H.I.E.L.D. and Hydra,
as well as Maria Hill's career move to Stark

00:07:27.449 --> 00:07:29.620
Industries, both happen in Winter Soldier.

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:34.740
We see the results of Stark's trauma in Iron
Man 3, which push him to create Ultron in

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:35.240
secret.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:39.680
Finally, Thor's knowledge of the Infinity
Stones in Ultron comes, in part, from his

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:41.700
experiences during The Dark World.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:46.320
The table is set for Age of Ultron's story,
so this one's placement is easy.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:52.220
As the final film of Phase Two, Ant-Man is
firmly set between Avengers: Age of Ultron

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:54.020
and Captain America: Civil War.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:58.780
We know it takes place after Age of Ultron,
partly because Scott tussles with Falcon in

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:03.380
full Avenger mode, and partly due to Hank
Pym's comments about the Battle of Sokovia.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.360
"Besides, they’re probably too busy dropping
cities out of the sky."

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:12.320
We also know it takes place right before Civil
War because Ant-Man ends with Scott learning

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:16.580
that Falcon is looking for him and in Civil
War we find out exactly why.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.560
There isn't anything too complicated to figure
out about the placement of Captain America:

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:22.080
Civil War.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.900
Just sit back and enjoy the straight continuity
of Scott Lang’s arc from Ant-Man, and the

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:30.840
return of Cap’s new Avengers team, last
seen at the end of Age of Ultron.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:36.600
What gets tricky, however, is what comes next
since Civil War introduces two new major heroes

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:40.850
to the MCU, who each need establishing along
with all the other characters Phase Three

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:42.200
brings to the franchise.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:46.900
Although it wasn’t released until around
halfway through Phase Three, Black Panther

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:51.880
really does belong right after Civil War since
the events of Tony and Steve’s feud lead

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:54.020
directly to the crisis in Wakanda.

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:58.440
While we've broken with strictly chronological
viewings of the franchise earlier on in this

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:00.700
list, we want to strongly emphasize it here.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:05.840
Few true Marvel Zombies would disagree that
waiting four films between Civil War and Black

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:10.460
Panther feels like far too long to wait to
see the next chapter in T'Challa's story.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:15.940
Like Black Panther, the events of Spider-Man:
Homecoming follow directly from those of Captain

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:20.010
America: Civil War and even revisits some
of Civil War during its prologue.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:21.860
"Okay, so the craziest thing just happened,
right?

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:25.840
I got in a fight with Captain America and
I stole his shield and I threw it at him and

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:27.960
I… uh, he's big now, I gotta go, hang on."

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:32.080
But it’s good to keep at least one movie
between Homecoming and Civil War, because

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:35.480
the end of Homecoming sees the return of Pepper
Potts to the franchise.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:40.060
During Civil War, we learned that Pepper and
Tony were "taking a break," while in Homecoming

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:42.000
they seem perfectly happy with one another.

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:46.440
And everyone knows that fractured relationships
heal in exactly the amount of time it takes

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:48.960
for a Wakandan succession crisis to be resolved.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:50.520
That's just common knowledge.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:56.340
After Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2 was released,
there was a lot of noise made by fans about

00:09:56.350 --> 00:10:01.370
about Stan Lee's cameo mentioning his Civil
War appearance, despite James Gunn’s insistence

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:03.880
that the movie took place before the events
of Civil War.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:05.000
Best thing to do here?

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:06.160
Don’t worry about it.

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:07.860
It takes place in space.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:09.660
None of the Avengers are in it.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:12.800
Forget about the nitty-gritty and just watch
Baby Groot dance.

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:17.890
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2 is best served
here not for any chronological reason, but

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:23.170
because there are only two movies left before
Infinity War, and one of those is Thor: Ragnarok.

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:28.310
Guardians Vol. 2 and Ragnarok are both great
fun, but you don’t really need to put two

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:30.600
witty, snarky, intergalactic epics back-to-back.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:35.400
There's an argument to be made to put this
movie closer to The Winter Soldier, since

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:40.520
Jasper Sitwell mentions Strange’s name in
a list of potential Hydra targets for Project:

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:42.320
Insight during the Captain America sequel:

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:48.160
"You, a TV anchor in Cairo, the undersecretary
of defense, a high school valedictorian in

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:51.590
Iowa City, Bruce Banner, Stephen Strange,
anyone who's a threat!"

00:10:51.590 --> 00:10:54.880
But that tease is pretty vague, and Hydra's
pretty evil.

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:57.620
Who knows why they're targeting a rich surgeon
in Manhattan?

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:02.260
So even though Sitwell's threat could place
this film earlier on our list, it's still

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:03.420
better off right here.

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:08.670
Doctor Strange's placement also breaks up
two space-based flicks: Guardians of the Galaxy

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:10.280
Vol. 2 and Thor: Ragnarok.

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:15.560
And considering Doctor Strange's mid-credits
scene is in fact a little slice of Ragnarok,

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:17.580
it's nice to get those two movies back-to-back.

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:23.220
When fans experienced Ragnarok's mid-credits
scene in theaters, it was difficult to imagine

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:26.260
that the looming shadow was from anything
but Thanos' ship.

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:31.380
At the time, that was an almost cruelly tantalizing
tease for the next step in Thor’s journey.

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:36.200
Now that Avengers: Infinity War has been released
and the brutal fate awaiting Loki, Heimdall,

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:40.490
and the other doomed passengers has become
clear, it feels a little strange not to watch

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:45.130
Ragnarok right before Infinity War, especially
considering the first thing audiences hear

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:48.880
in Infinity War is the Asgardian refugee ship's
distress call.

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:53.820
Sticking with Marvel Studios' order of release
which puts Black Panther after Ragnarok just

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:55.260
doesn’t make a whole lot of sense.

00:11:56.120 --> 00:12:00.840
Marvel Studios put some unbelievable time,
effort, and money into the build-up to Avengers:

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:04.360
Infinity War, and you have to believe they
had a reason to release it when they did.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:08.920
Yes, they may have put fans through torture
by putting two movies between Infinity War

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:13.020
and its resolution but, by this point, Marvel
is at the top of its game.

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:15.580
The studio's not going to steer you wrong,
here.

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:20.440
Trust in Feige, and try to remember that there’s
probably no good reason to move Infinity War

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:23.540
any closer to its conclusion in Avengers:
Endgame.

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:28.320
For the most part, Ant-Man and the Wasp seems
to take place at roughly the same time as

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:29.180
Infinity War.

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:33.330
And in the mid-credits scene we see the latter
movie’s devastating effects take hold on

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:34.720
Scott Lang’s world.

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:38.560
"Hank, quit screwing around, you told me yourself
not to screw around.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:40.460
Hank!

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:42.920
Hope?"

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:47.000
But don’t be tempted to move Ant-Man and
the Wasp to an earlier place in your viewing

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:47.500
order.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:52.220
Not only does this movie give away what happens
during Infinity War, but it also provides

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:56.240
some much-needed laughter after the massive
downer that was Infinity War’s ending.

00:12:56.340 --> 00:12:58.240
Why take the chaser before the shot?

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:03.771
Because Captain Marvel makes such a point
of its ‘90s setting, those arguing for a

00:13:03.771 --> 00:13:07.550
strictly chronological viewing would happily
move this movie all the way to the beginning

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:08.540
of Phase One.

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:13.000
But doing that will seriously affect your
enjoyment of Avengers: Endgame.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:17.120
The first meeting between Carol Danvers and
the Avengers is sure to be a huge moment for

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:17.940
the franchise.

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:22.800
If Marvel had told these stories chronologically,
however, and released Captain Marvel twenty

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:26.630
films ago without mentioning her again since,
would that still be the case?

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:31.000
Think of it this way: there’s a reason why
Pulp Fiction earned its place in cinema history.

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:35.620
Sometimes, for a story to make any kind of
sense, you have to tell it out of order.

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:39.940
And the fact that this franchise is as good
as it is… well, Nick Fury himself thinks

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:41.960
it could even be divine intervention:

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:44.700
"What happened here was a miracle!

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:46.340
And I want you to f------ acknowledge it!"

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:48.740
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:52.500
Plus, even more Looper videos about the Marvel
